2026-03-18T00:10:01 [DEBUG] openai._base_client: Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3e662568-0fc7-465c-8bce-831da9dd3445', 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'developer', 'content': '[GENERAL_LAYER v1.1]\n\nYou are a senior technical recruiter and resume strategist specializing in software engineering roles.\n\nINPUTS (provided in separate messages):\n- CANDIDATE_PROFILE (verified background details; may include leadership scope, domains, constraints)\n- JOB_DESCRIPTION (role requirements and company context)\n- MASTER_RESUME (source of truth for employment history, titles, dates, and factual claims)\n- MASTER_COVER_LETTER (source of truth for writing style and baseline structure)\n- TASK (company + role + special instructions)\n\nPRIMARY GOAL\nGenerate a tailored resume and cover letter optimized for the specific role in TASK, while preserving factual accuracy and integrity.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT\nReturn JSON only with exactly these keys:\n{\n  "resume": "...",\n  "cover_letter": "..."\n}\n\nHARD CONSTRAINTS (truth / safety)\n1) Do NOT invent employers, titles, dates, products, technologies, tools, metrics, or responsibilities not supported by MASTER_RESUME or CANDIDATE_PROFILE.\n2) Strengthen impact framing and clarity, but do NOT fabricate numbers or outcomes.\n3) No invented causality: do not add "resulting in / leading to / enabling" unless the causal link is explicitly supported by the source text.\n4) Evidence constraint: each rewritten bullet must be traceable to a source bullet and/or a specific statement in CANDIDATE_PROFILE.\n5) Maintain reverse chronological order of roles; do not reorder roles.\n6) You may reorder bullet points WITHIN a role.\n7) Bullet integrity: each bullet should describe one coherent initiative; do not merge unrelated achievements into one bullet.\n8) Do not echo/paraphrase JOB_DESCRIPTION responsibilities unless the candidate demonstrably performed them; when not supported, emphasize adjacent transferable experience without implying direct execution.\n9) Preserve seniority honesty: do not inflate scope (e.g., "owned company-wide strategy", "executive stakeholder management", "P&L ownership") unless explicitly supported.\n\nSECTION ORDER CONSTRAINT (STRICT)\n\nThe resume MUST preserve the same top-level section order as MASTER_RESUME.\n\nDo NOT reorder, rename, or move sections.\n\nRequired section order:\n\n1. Professional Summary\n2. Experience\n3. Education\n4. Technical Skills\n5. Additional (if present)\n\nRules:\n- You may modify bullet wording within sections.\n- You may reorder bullets inside a role.\n- You may add or remove bullets within a role.\n- You may reorganize categories inside Technical Skills.\n\nHowever:\n- Do NOT move Technical Skills above Experience.\n- Do NOT move Education.\n- Do NOT introduce new top-level sections.\n\nTAILORING REQUIREMENTS (must do)\n10) Extract the 5 most critical competency themes from JOB_DESCRIPTION (e.g., leadership, SaaS/cloud, delivery excellence, AI adoption, security/compliance).\n11) Ensure those themes are reflected across:\n    - Professional Summary\n    - Experience bullets (especially latest 2-3 roles)\n    - Technical Skills (reordered and renamed categories allowed)\n12) Avoid cosmetic-only edits. Changes must materially improve role fit by:\n    - shifting emphasis toward relevant initiatives\n    - improving seniority framing where supported\n    - foregrounding domain-relevant scope\n13) Reduce or compress content that is not relevant to the role (especially older roles), without deleting important credibility signals.\n\nSTYLE / QUALITY\n14) Prefer concise, high-signal bullets. Start with action verbs. Keep tense consistent (past for past roles, present for current).\n15) Avoid buzzword stuffing. Use job-relevant terminology only when grounded in demonstrated experience.\n16) Remove redundant phrases and filler ("responsible for", "worked on").\n17) Keep the resume readable by ATS: plain text, consistent headings, no tables.\n\nSKILLS SECTION NORMALIZATION (validator-friendly, still truthful)\n18) If you are instructed to include a "required skill" that is already supported by MASTER_RESUME or CANDIDATE_PROFILE, you MUST include the exact string verbatim somewhere in the Technical Skills section.\n19) If a required skill does not cleanly fit an existing category, add it under:\n    Other: <comma-separated list of items>\n20) If a required skill is NOT supported by MASTER_RESUME or CANDIDATE_PROFILE, do NOT include it (do not guess). Instead, omit it and keep the skills section honest.\n\nCOVER LETTER RULES\n21) Must explicitly mention the company + role from TASK.\n22) Must connect: candidate background -> company needs -> role responsibilities, using only supported claims.\n23) Keep it 3-5 short paragraphs; be specific but not verbose.\n\n[CANDIDATE_LAYER_OLI_TREADWELL v1.0]\n\nCORE POSITIONING ANCHORS\n- Position as: Senior Software Engineer (10+ years) specializing in modern web application development with strong front-end architecture and full-stack delivery.\n- Emphasize: building accessible, user-friendly interfaces; component-driven UI engineering; pragmatic collaboration across design/engineering.\n- Anchor stack: React + TypeScript + Node.js with Rails/PostgreSQL experience; testing discipline (Jest/RSpec) and UI system tooling (Storybook).\n- Prefer role targeting language: “Senior/Staff-level UI or Full-Stack Engineer (web)” rather than “distributed systems/backend platform” unless the job explicitly matches and evidence exists.\n\nARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENTIATION (MANDATORY SURFACING)\n- Surface explicitly (when relevant to the target role):\n  - Re-architected a Rewards platform from Rails-rendered UI to ReactJS (treat as a front-end architecture modernization; do not imply a full backend rewrite unless supported).\n  - Built/maintained a modular design system to drive UI consistency and reuse (component library + standards).\n  - Componentization and reuse: reusable React/TypeScript components for HR management workflows.\n- Ensure at least one bullet in the most recent/most prominent role includes a concrete architecture mechanism: “migrated UI architecture from Rails to React”, “introduced Storybook-driven component library”, “standardized design tokens/components”, etc.\n\nARCHITECTURE SIGNAL PRIORITIZATION\n- Prioritize bullets that show:\n  1) Concrete UI/platform architecture mechanisms (migration approach, modular component system, design system governance).\n  2) Quality mechanisms (testing strategy with Jest/Chai/RSpec; CI usage with CircleCI) tied to delivery reliability.\n  3) Cross-functional execution with design tools (Figma/Zeplin) only when tied to implementation mechanisms (handoff workflow, component specs).\n  4) Leadership (code review, delegation, coaching) as supporting evidence, not the main story.\n- Prefer rewriting achievements to expose the mechanism behind “15% engagement increase” (what changed in UI/UX architecture) without inventing details.\n\nDISTRIBUTED SYSTEM DEPTH\n- Do NOT force distributed-systems framing; only include if MASTER_RESUME provides explicit evidence (queues, caching, replicas, sharding, idempotency, tracing, etc.).\n- If a job description demands distributed systems, translate cautiously:\n  - Emphasize “web application architecture, API integration, performance-minded UI” rather than claiming backend scaling mechanisms not present in evidence.\n- Avoid terms like “high-throughput”, “low-latency”, “microservices”, “event-driven” unless explicitly supported by evidence sources.\n\nTECHNICAL DIFFERENTIATION\n- Emphasize concrete strengths supported by evidence:\n  - ReactJS + TypeScript UI architecture; component libraries; Storybook workflows.\n  - NodeJS and Ruby on Rails full-stack delivery; GraphQL usage (only as “worked with GraphQL” unless deeper ownership is evidenced).\n  - PostgreSQL via Rails/ActiveRecord (avoid claiming DBA-level work).\n  - Testing: Jest/Chai (JS) and RSpec (Rails); prefer mechanism-first quality bullets.\n  - CI: CircleCI (state as used/maintained pipelines only if evidenced).\n- Prefer mechanism-first bullet phrasing:\n  - “Introduced Storybook-based component documentation to standardize UI patterns…” (only if supported).\n  - “Migrated Rails-rendered views to React components to enable modular UI development…” (safe given architecture_patterns).\n\nDOMAIN / INDUSTRY FRAMING\n- Primary domain credibility: Human Resources software, rewards/engagement platforms, education-related web products; secondary exposure to museums.\n- Frame domains as product contexts, not specialized compliance/regulatory expertise.\n- Prefer: “HR management workflows” / “rewards engagement UI” rather than niche HR/legal claims.\n\nSAFE DOMAIN TRANSLATION\n- Translate domain experience to broadly applicable web-product value:\n  - HR management → complex internal/admin workflows, forms, permissions-sensitive UI (do not claim auth implementation unless evidenced).\n  - Rewards platform → consumer-facing engagement features and UI iteration tied to measurable engagement outcomes.\n  - Education/museums → content-rich experiences and usability/accessibility emphasis (only if supported by MASTER_RESUME).\n- Keep translations conservative: focus on UI architecture, component reuse, and delivery practices.\n\nDOMAIN MISINTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS\n- Do NOT claim specialization in:\n  - Security/compliance frameworks (SOC2, HIPAA, GDPR) unless explicitly in evidence.\n  - Deep backend/platform engineering (microservices, Kubernetes, Kafka, Redis, etc.) unless explicitly in evidence.\n  - Data engineering/ETL pipelines or ML/AI systems.\n- Do NOT invent scale metrics (traffic, users, latency) or migration details (e.g., “strangler pattern”, “micro-frontend”) unless present in evidence.\n\nEXTENSIBILITY AND INTEGRATION POSITIONING\n- Reinterpret “design system” and “reusable components” as extensibility signals:\n  - “Modular component architecture enabling faster feature delivery and consistent UI.”\n  - “Shared UI primitives/patterns used across multiple product areas.”\n- For integration language, stay within evidence:\n  - It is safe to mention “GraphQL integration” only as usage; do not claim schema ownership, federation, or gateway architecture unless supported.\n- Emphasize maintainability mechanisms: component boundaries, consistent styling (SASS/SCSS), testing, and review standards.\n\nLEADERSHIP FRAMING\n- Frame leadership as hands-on technical leadership:\n  - Delegated ownership of components/features; set expectations via code review.\n  - Mentored/coached junior engineers through 1:1s and feedback loops.\n  - Protected production stability by pushing back on risky changes; describe as “risk-based decisioning” without dramatization.\n- Prefer leadership evidence tied to mechanisms:\n  - “Established code review norms for React/TypeScript components…” (only if supported).\n  - “Coached juniors on testing practices (Jest/RSpec)…” (only if supported).\n\nLEADERSHIP BALANCE FOR SENIOR ROLE\n- Keep narrative execution-heavy:\n  - 70–85% technical architecture + delivery bullets; 15–30% leadership/process.\n- Avoid sounding like an engineering manager unless the target role is EM and evidence supports it.\n- Leadership should reinforce impact (quality, maintainability, delivery speed), not replace technical depth.\n\nAI WORK POSITIONING\n- No AI tooling or AI project evidence is provided.\n- Do NOT mention AI/LLMs, prompt engineering, model evaluation, or AI-driven features unless present in MASTER_RESUME or explicitly added evidence.\n\nCLAIM BOUNDARIES / PROHIBITED INFLATION\n- Do not claim:\n  - Backend re-architecture beyond “UI/front-end migration from Rails to React” unless evidence shows backend changes.\n  - Security/auth implementation, compliance, or privacy programs (none evidenced).\n  - DevOps/infra ownership (no infra stack evidenced).\n  - Observability systems (no metrics/logging/tracing tooling evidenced).\n- Do not fabricate:\n  - Additional metrics beyond “15% increase in customer engagement” and “delivered reusable components.”\n  - Team size, timelines, budgets, or scope.\n- When referencing tools (Figma/Zeplin/JIRA/GitHub), keep them as collaboration/tooling context, not primary achievements.\n\nEVIDENCE SOURCES\n- Only use:\n  - MASTER_RESUME (if provided at generation time)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.summary/headline\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.experience_highlights (areas, impacts, skills_applied, architecture_patterns)\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.technical_skills\n  - CANDIDATE_PROFILE.leadership (practices + risk_management)\n- Bullet construction rules:\n  - Every mechanism must be traceable to an evidence source above.\n  - If a mechanism is not explicitly evidenced, phrase conservatively (“worked with”, “contributed to”) or omit.\n\nFINAL REMINDER\n- Core differentiation to keep visible: senior-level React/TypeScript UI architecture + design-system-driven component reuse, with full-stack fluency (Node/Rails/Postgres) and pragmatic technical leadership focused on quality and stability.\n\n[ROLE_LEVEL_TUNING v2.0]\n\nYou will receive role_level.\n\nrole_level is one of:\ndirector, manager, principal, staff, senior, mid, junior.\n\nApply guidance to BOTH resume and cover letter.\n\nGENERAL RULES\n- Prefer concrete artifacts and outcomes over adjectives.\n- Avoid generic phrases unless followed by specific system or process detail.\n- Avoid stacking multiple mechanisms in one sentence.\n- Avoid vague claims like “mission-critical systems” without specifics.\n- Do not inflate scope beyond evidence.\n\nGENERIC LANGUAGE SUPPRESSION\nDo not use:\n- scalable platform\n- robust system\n- cutting-edge\n- innovative solution\nunless followed by specific mechanism and measurable outcome.\n\nMECHANISM DEPTH RULE\nEvery mechanism bullet must include:\n- The mechanism itself\n- The system boundary or component it affected\n- A constraint, tradeoff, or context\n- A measurable or observable outcome (if supported)\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nDIRECTOR\n\nFocus:\n- Organizational impact\n- Strategy\n- Cross-team coordination\n- Delivery governance\n\nArtifact Enforcement (MANDATORY if supported by evidence):\nInclude at least 3 of the following artifact types across the resume:\n\n1) Delivery Governance Artifact\n   - Release cadence definition\n   - Process transformation\n   - Quality or predictability improvement\n   - Escalation framework\n   - Risk management mechanism\n\n2) Organizational Leadership Artifact\n   - Hiring decisions or headcount growth\n   - Team structure evolution\n   - Mentorship or leadership development program\n   - Performance management system\n\n3) Cross-Functional Alignment Artifact\n   - Roadmap alignment with Product\n   - Executive reporting\n   - Dependency coordination across teams\n   - Stakeholder negotiation\n\n4) Strategic Technical Direction Artifact\n   - Platform standardization\n   - Architectural direction setting\n   - Long-term modernization initiative\n   - Governance of shared components\n\nRules:\n- Do not describe leadership abstractly.\n- At least one artifact must include measurable impact or observable change.\n- Mechanisms must support strategic direction, not implementation detail.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMANAGER\n\nFocus:\n- Team execution\n- Delivery ownership\n- People development\n- Operational reliability\n\nArtifact Enforcement (MANDATORY if supported by evidence):\nInclude at least 3 of the following artifact types across the resume:\n\n1) Delivery Execution Artifact\n   - Sprint planning ownership\n   - Milestone delivery\n   - Incident leadership\n   - Backlog prioritization\n   - Reduction of spillover or missed commitments\n\n2) People Development Artifact\n   - Hiring participation\n   - Mentoring engineers\n   - Performance reviews\n   - Career growth guidance\n   - Onboarding improvements\n\n3) Cross-Functional Coordination Artifact\n   - Collaboration with Product or Design\n   - Stakeholder updates\n   - Risk communication\n   - Requirement clarification cycles\n\n4) Operational Improvement Artifact\n   - Process optimization\n   - Test coverage increase\n   - Deployment pipeline improvement\n   - Observability or reliability enhancement\n\nRules:\n- At least one artifact must show execution accountability (not just participation).\n- At least one artifact must show people leadership.\n- Mechanisms should reinforce delivery credibility, not dominate.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPRINCIPAL\n\nFocus:\n- Architecture standards\n- Cross-team influence\n- Technical leadership without direct org management\n\nArtifact Expectations:\nInclude at least 2 of:\n- Architecture review process\n- Cross-team standard adoption\n- Design RFC leadership\n- System boundary clarification\n- Platform-level design tradeoff\n\nMechanisms must illustrate architectural direction.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSTAFF\n\nFocus:\n- High-leverage execution\n- Mentorship\n- Influence within team or domain\n\nInclude at least:\n- 1 cross-team contribution\n- 1 technical depth artifact\n- 1 reliability or scalability artifact\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSENIOR\n\nFocus:\n- Strong technical ownership\n- Performance and reliability impact\n\nInclude:\n- 1 significant technical initiative\n- 1 performance or reliability improvement\n- 1 collaboration signal\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMID / JUNIOR\n\nFocus:\n- Execution quality\n- Learning velocity\n- Growing ownership\n\nMechanisms framed as contribution, not strategy.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nFINAL CHECK\n- Tone matches role_level.\n- Artifacts are concrete, not abstract.\n- No scope inflation.\n- Mechanisms are contextual and relevant.\n- Leadership claims are supported by operational evidence.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'CANDIDATE_PROFILE:\n{\n  "domains": {\n    "primary": [\n      "Human Resources",\n      "Auto parts and repair",\n      "Education"\n    ],\n    "secondary": [\n      "Museums"\n    ]\n  },\n  "candidate": {\n    "name": "Oli Treadwell",\n    "summary": "Senior Software Engineer with 10+ years of expertise using modern web development technologies. Proven ability \\nto build accessible and user-friendly interfaces, collaborate effectively within teams, and deliver high-quality code. \\nSeeking software engineering roles at any level.",\n    "headline": "Senior Software Engineer"\n  },\n  "leadership": {\n    "scope": {\n      "team_size_max": 10,\n      "style_keywords": [\n        "Leadership by example",\n        "Consensus vs authority"\n      ]\n    },\n    "practices": [\n      "Delegating ownership",\n      "Code review",\n      "Ownership culture",\n      "One-on-one coaching",\n      "Junior Engineers Management"\n    ],\n    "risk_management": [\n      "Pushed back on risky changes that threaten production stability",\n      "Calculated risk acceptance"\n    ]\n  },\n  "role_fit_themes": [],\n  "claim_boundaries": {\n    "domain_limits": [],\n    "security_auth": [],\n    "employment_constraints": []\n  },\n  "technical_skills": {\n    "languages": [\n      "NodeJS",\n      "TypeScript",\n      "Ruby on Rails"\n    ],\n    "datastores": [\n      "PostgreSQL"\n    ],\n    "api_patterns": [],\n    "infra_devops": [],\n    "observability": [],\n    "async_messaging": [],\n    "backend_systems": [],\n    "security_auth_patterns": [],\n    "scalability_reliability_patterns": []\n  },\n  "ai_tooling_practice": {\n    "principles": [],\n    "hands_on_tools": [],\n    "usage_patterns": [],\n    "concepts_familiarity": []\n  },\n  "experience_highlights": [\n    {\n      "area": "Rewards platform",\n      "impact": [\n        "A 15% increase in customer engagement"\n      ],\n      "market": null,\n      "team_context": [],\n      "skills_applied": [\n        "ReactJS",\n        "Storybook",\n        "NodeJS",\n        "Jest",\n        "Chai",\n        "TypeScript",\n        "Ruby on Rails",\n        "ActiveRecord",\n        "RSpec",\n        "GraphQL",\n        "PostgreSQL",\n        "Circle CI",\n        "SASS/SCSS",\n        "GitHub",\n        "Design Language Systems",\n        "JIRA",\n        "Zeplin",\n        "Figma"\n      ],\n      "architecture_patterns": [\n        "Re-architected the Rewards platform from Rails to ReactJS",\n        "Modular design system",\n        "UI consistency"\n      ],\n      "employer_relationship": null,\n      "security_auth_patterns": [],\n      "constraints_and_tradeoffs": []\n    },\n    {\n      "area": "Human Resources Management",\n      "impact": [\n        "Delivered reusable React/TypeScript components"\n      ],\n      "market": null,\n      "team_context": [],\n      "skills_applied": [],\n      "architecture_patterns": [],\n      "employer_relationship": null,\n      "security_auth_patterns": [],\n      "constraints_and_tradeoffs": []\n    }\n  ],\n  "candidate_profile_version": "2.0",\n  "constraints_and_preferences": {\n    "work_context": [],\n    "communication": [],\n    "resume_constraint": []\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "JOB_DESCRIPTION:\nTechnology - Ontario, Canada\n        \n            London,\n            Ontario\n\n\nDescription\n\n\n\n\nJoin Team CARFAX as a \nSenior Software Engineer - Team Lead\xa0\xa0\n\xa0\nIsn't it time you bragged about where you work? At CARFAX, we do, every day. We pride ourselves on being mission-focused on helping to grow a brand built on accuracy and integrity. We care deeply about our products and our customers. We’re more than just a company: We help millions of consumers make more-informed decisions every day. We know that our teammates are our most valuable asset, and we value a balanced life while tackling challenging projects in a fast-paced environment. One last thing: Our four-day work week continues in Summer 2026!\nWhat makes our Tech Culture Amazing?\xa0\n\xa0\nWe’re passionate about having a creative and innovative environment where our developers collaborate and continuously learn and grow. Our developers have the freedom to bring new tools, frameworks, and ideas to the table knowing their voice will be heard. We’re not afraid of trying new things; if the case is made for a new technology or approach, our developers know it will be tried. Within CARFAX, we value Teamwork. Our development teams work hand in hand with product owners, project leaders, UX designers and Quality Assurance engineers to produce the best products and solutions possible. If you’re a techie who enjoys constantly learning new things, collaborating with teammates, and building best-in-class products and services, you’ll find exciting opportunities at CARFAX!\xa0\xa0\nAs a \nSenior Software Engineer – Team Lead,\n you will be part of the Account Engineering department within Product Engineering. Account Engineering is a team of 30+ tech savvy individuals who work together every day to deliver customer facing authentication solutions. This team is responsible for making logging in and signing up at CARFAX a secure, consistent, and high-quality experience for CARFAX customers, as well as managing user profiles, Pll data and compliance, CARFAX websites' global header and footer, Universal Car Garage, user behavioural tracking, and user personalization for messaging and front-end experiences. This includes managing and customizing third party authentication solutions while also building and maintaining custom authentication solutions to provide our customers with a great experience that fits our needs!\xa0\xa0\nAt CARFAX, we believe in the power of teamwork and value in-person interactions so that we can collaborate and thrive together. This position will require 2 days per week in our London, ON office subject to change with future business needs.\nA typical day will have you:\xa0\xa0\nWorking in a dynamic, agile, matrix environment supporting high-use, modern applications\nMentor team members by offering regular feedback on code, team performance and growth opportunities.\nLead design decisions, including new technology research and prototyping\nCollaborating closely with other developers, Architects, UX designers, Project and Product Managers, support teams and other stakeholders\nPromoting high quality and adequate software design through a disciplined development methodology\nAugmenting and adjusting team processes and practices towards continuous improvement\nDocumenting processes, architecture, maintenance, actions and decisions\nEnsuring adequate NFRs are implemented and updated throughout the application lifecycle\nRequired Experience:\nAbility to provide leadership and mentor engineers through complicated technical challenges\nAbility to give team members opportunities to grow by delegating tasks and not being a “superhero”\nAbility to give team members feedback and coaching on technical skill gaps necessary for growth\nAbility to work and collaborate effectively in a pod-matrix environment as well as independently\nExperience with modern front-end JavaScript libraries (such as ReactJS, Vue, or Angular)\nExperience with JavaScript, NodeJS, TypeScript, HTML5 and CSS3\nExperience with scripting languages such as Bash\nExperience with Continuous Integration / Delivery such as Gitlab CI/CD\nExperience with Containerization (Docker)\nExperience with Object oriented concepts and languages such as TypeScript , Java, Kotlin\nExperience with Relational and NoSQL database systems such as MySQL, DynamoDB, Elasticsearch\nExperience with developing in a cloud environment (AWS preferred)\nExperience in full SDLC development including design, building, testing, deployment, upgrades and support\nExperience with application monitoring and alerting systems and standards (such as New Relic, OTEL, CloudWatch, ITIL, Prometheus, PagerDuty)\nExperience with being On Call and its general expectation\nExperience with AI Coding tools (such as GitHub Copilot)\nKnowledge of client/server architecture and building APIs and web services\nKnowledge of coding best practices for security and performance\nLoves to learn! To get the best solutions we need to continually explore what’s new and be willing to dive in and learn\nPreferred Experience:\xa0\xa0\nExperience with Authentication / Authorization systems\nExperience with microservice architecture\nExperience with SEO and/or mobile development\nKnowledge of Full Stack software development \nBuilt large-scale, common applications or services that enrich multiple products\nSupported applications in multiple markets and time zones and languages (e.g. Canada)\nAgile software development including Test Driven Development and Pair Programming\nExperience with OAuth2.0, OIDC, or other modern authentication frameworks and conventions\nExperience with A/B testing \nBonus Experience: \nExperience and curiosity with AI/ML concepts and tooling (AWS preferred)\nDon’t just take our word for it:\xa0\n\xa0\n10X Virginia Business Best Places to Work\xa0\n9X Washingtonian Great Places to Work\xa0\n10X Washington Post Top Workplace\xa0\n3XSt. Louis Post-Dispatch Best Places to Work\xa0\nWhat’s in it for you:\xa0\xa0\nCompetitive compensation, benefits and generous time-off policies\n4-Day summer work weeks and a winter holiday break\n401(k) / DCPP matching\nAnnual bonus program\nCasual, dog-friendly, and innovative office spaces\nFor a comprehensive list of benefits, please visit our website:\xa0\nhttps://jobs.jobvite.com/carfax/p/benefits\n.\n\n\nAbout CARFAX and S&P Global Mobility\n\nS&P Global has recently \nannounced\n the intent to separate our Mobility Segment into a standalone public company.\n\n\n\nCARFAX\n, part of S&P Global Mobility, helps millions of people every day confidently shop, buy, service and sell used cars with innovative solutions powered by CARFAX vehicle history information. The expert in vehicle history since 1984, CARFAX provides exclusive services like CARFAX Used Car Listings, CARFAX Car Care, CARFAX History-Based Value and the flagship CARFAX® Vehicle History Report™ to consumers and the automotive industry. CARFAX owns the world’s largest vehicle history database and is nationally recognized as a top workplace by The Washington Post and Glassdoor.com. Shop, Buy, Service, Sell – Show me the CARFAX™. S&P Global Mobility is a division of S&P Global (NYSE: SPGI). S&P Global is the world’s foremost provider of credit ratings, benchmarks, analytics and workflow solutions in the global capital, commodity and automotive markets. \n\n\nUS Equal Opportunity Employer Statement:\n\nCARFAX is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. It is the policy of CARFAX to provide equal employment opportunity to all persons regardless of race, color, sex, pregnancy, religion, national origin, age, ancestry, citizenship status, veteran status, military status, disability or handicap, sexual orientation, genetic information or any other status protected by federal, state or local law. In addition, CARFAX will provide reasonable accommodations for qualified individuals with disabilities. We maintain a drug-free workplace. We are a participant in E-Verify.\n\n\nCanadian Equal Opportunity Employer Statement:\n\nCARFAX Canada is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race/ethnicity, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, disability, marital status, military veteran status, unemployment status, or any other status protected by law.\n\n\nWe’re committed to providing accommodations by request for candidates taking part in all aspects of the recruitment and selection process. For a confidential inquiry or to request an accommodation, please contact your recruiter or email \n[email\xa0protected]\n."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'MASTER_RESUME:\nOli Treadwell  SUMMARYSenior Software Engineer with 10+ years of experience building modern web applicationswith React and TypeScript, plus full-stack delivery with Node.js and Ruby on Rails. Knownfor accessible, user-friendly UI, component-driven architecture (design systems/Storybook),and disciplined testing practices. Hands-on technical leader who mentors engineers throughcomplex work via delegation, code review, and coaching while protecting productionstability. PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCESenior Software Engineer | Numeral Studio | Wellington, NZ | 2025– Present• Delivered reusable React/TypeScript components inside Electron with accessible,responsive UI patterns plus Jest/React Testing Library coverage so compliancereports went in without manual errors.• Built React/TypeScript interfaces and Prisma-backed Node/Express services for secureHR case workflows, adding dynamic staff routing, hardened permissions, andautomated ESLint/Prettier + unit-test checks.• Supported the launch of the design conference 2025 and 2026 site onNext.js/TypeScript with Sanity CMS and Storybook, extending the design system tocut duplicate code and keep the experience polished.• Contributed to a mobile-first interactive marketing demo for a Fortune 500 progressiveweb app, handling localized feature work with remote teams and tuning performancefor fast global launches.• Technologies: React, Next.js, Electron, TypeScript, SCSS, Node.js/Express, PrismaORM, Sanity CMS, Storybook, GSAP, Cypress, Jest, React Testing Library, GitHubActions, ESLint, Prettier, localization & accessibility tools.Software Engineer (Contract) | PartsTrader Markets Ltd. |Wellington, NZ | 2024 – 2025• Developed a dedicated .NET microservice to interface with a legacy API and built amodern, responsive front-end using React with TypeScript.• Translated Figma UI designs into dynamic, accessible interfaces using React,TypeScript, and styled-components.• Applied Domain-Driven Design principles by structuring the microservice withRepository, Domain Model, Mapper, and Application Service patterns.• Technologies: .NET service layer, React, TypeScript, Cypress, GitHub Actions,domain-driven design patterns.\nOli Treadwell\nSUMMARY\nSenior Software Engineer with 10+ years of experience building modern web applications\nwith React and TypeScript, plus full-stack delivery with Node.js and Ruby on Rails. Known\nfor accessible, user-friendly UI, component-driven architecture (design systems/Storybook),\nand disciplined testing practices. Hands-on technical leader who mentors engineers through\ncomplex work via delegation, code review, and coaching while protecting production\nstability.\nPROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE\nSenior Software Engineer | Numeral Studio | Wellington, NZ | 2025\n– Present\n• Delivered reusable React/TypeScript components inside Electron with accessible,\nresponsive UI patterns plus Jest/React Testing Library coverage so compliance\nreports went in without manual errors.\n• Built React/TypeScript interfaces and Prisma-backed Node/Express services for secure\nHR case workflows, adding dynamic staff routing, hardened permissions, and\nautomated ESLint/Prettier + unit-test checks.\n• Supported the launch of the design conference 2025 and 2026 site on\nNext.js/TypeScript with Sanity CMS and Storybook, extending the design system to\ncut duplicate code and keep the experience polished.\n• Contributed to a mobile-first interactive marketing demo for a Fortune 500 progressive\nweb app, handling localized feature work with remote teams and tuning performance\nfor fast global launches.\n• Technologies: React, Next.js, Electron, TypeScript, SCSS, Node.js/Express, Prisma\nORM, Sanity CMS, Storybook, GSAP, Cypress, Jest, React Testing Library, GitHub\nActions, ESLint, Prettier, localization & accessibility tools.\nSoftware Engineer (Contract) | PartsTrader Markets Ltd. |\nWellington, NZ | 2024 – 2025\n• Developed a dedicated .NET microservice to interface with a legacy API and built a\nmodern, responsive front-end using React with TypeScript.\n• Translated Figma UI designs into dynamic, accessible interfaces using React,\nTypeScript, and styled-components.\n• Applied Domain-Driven Design principles by structuring the microservice with\nRepository, Domain Model, Mapper, and Application Service patterns.\n• Technologies: .NET service layer, React, TypeScript, Cypress, GitHub Actions,\ndomain-driven design patterns.\nSoftware Engineer & Founder | Habitual Genesis | Remote | 2016 –Present• Partner with authors, museums, and education nonprofits to ship low-maintenancedigital products.• Delivered discovery-through-launch engagements while keeping ongoing costs low.• Stayed lean by embedding with stakeholders to clarify needs, prioritise MVPs, and trialmarketing ideas without raising spend.• Various projects with General Assembly, Thinkful, Codecademy, and others• Technologies: JavaScript, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap, GitHub, GitHub Pages,Squarespace, Mailchimp, Google Drive.Software Engineer & Lead Instructor | The Flatiron School |Remote | 2022 – 2023• Delivered remote & asynchronous technical training on software engineering to adiverse group of students.• Wrote and presented curriculum covering JavaScript, HTML/CSS, ReactJS, Python,Flask, PostgreSQL, Object-Oriented Programming, CI/CD, Agile, APIs, WebAccessibility, and more.• Mentored and coached junior engineers, preparing them for success in their roles andjob duties.• Lead a working group promoting inclusive web design and web accessibility practices.• Technologies: JavaScript, HTML5, CSS3, ReactJS, React Router, Python, Flask,Object-Oriented Programming, CI/CD, Render.com, Agile, APIs, Web AccessibilitySenior Software Engineer | WorkTango (formerly KazooHR) |Remote | 2019 – 2020Software Engineer | Union Metrics | Austin, TX | 2014 – 2016Software Engineer (Contract) | Bypass Mobile | Austin, TX | 2016 –2016Software Engineer & Instructor | Hack Reactor (formerlyGalvanize) | Austin, TX | 2016 – 2017Software Engineer | Condé Nast | Austin, TX | 2017 – 2019• Re-architected the Rewards platform from Rails to ReactJS, resulting in a 15% increasein customer engagement.• Implemented a modular design system to improve UI consistency and boost developervelocity.• Integrated features across Performance Management and Rewards platforms tooverhaul the product suite.• Technologies: ReactJS, Storybook, NodeJS, Jest, Chai, TypeScript, Ruby on Rails,ActiveRecord, RSpec, GraphQL, PostgreSQL, Circle CI, SASS/SCSS, GitHub,Design Language Systems, JIRA, Zeplin, Figma.Senior Software Engineer \nSoftware Engineer & Founder | Habitual Genesis | Remote | 2016 –\nPresent\n• Partner with authors, museums, and education nonprofits to ship low-maintenance\ndigital products.\n• Delivered discovery-through-launch engagements while keeping ongoing costs low.\n• Stayed lean by embedding with stakeholders to clarify needs, prioritise MVPs, and trial\nmarketing ideas without raising spend.\n• Various projects with General Assembly, Thinkful, Codecademy, and others\n• Technologies: JavaScript, HTML5, CSS3, Bootstrap, GitHub, GitHub Pages,\nSquarespace, Mailchimp, Google Drive.\nSoftware Engineer & Lead Instructor | The Flatiron School |\nRemote | 2022 – 2023\n• Delivered remote & asynchronous technical training on software engineering to a\ndiverse group of students.\n• Wrote and presented curriculum covering JavaScript, HTML/CSS, ReactJS, Python,\nFlask, PostgreSQL, Object-Oriented Programming, CI/CD, Agile, APIs, Web\nAccessibility, and more.\n• Mentored and coached junior engineers, preparing them for success in their roles and\njob duties.\n• Lead a working group promoting inclusive web design and web accessibility practices.\n• Technologies: JavaScript, HTML5, CSS3, ReactJS, React Router, Python, Flask,\nObject-Oriented Programming, CI/CD, Render.com, Agile, APIs, Web Accessibility\nSenior Software Engineer | WorkTango (formerly KazooHR) |\nRemote | 2019 – 2020\nSoftware Engineer | Union Metrics | Austin, TX | 2014 – 2016\nSoftware Engineer (Contract) | Bypass Mobile | Austin, TX | 2016 –\n2016\nSoftware Engineer & Instructor | Hack Reactor (formerly\nGalvanize) | Austin, TX | 2016 – 2017\nSoftware Engineer | Condé Nast | Austin, TX | 2017 – 2019\n• Re-architected the Rewards platform from Rails to ReactJS, resulting in a 15% increase\nin customer engagement.\n• Implemented a modular design system to improve UI consistency and boost developer\nvelocity.\n• Integrated features across Performance Management and Rewards platforms to\noverhaul the product suite.\n• Technologies: ReactJS, Storybook, NodeJS, Jest, Chai, TypeScript, Ruby on Rails,\nActiveRecord, RSpec, GraphQL, PostgreSQL, Circle CI, SASS/SCSS, GitHub,\nDesign Language Systems, JIRA, Zeplin, Figma.\nSenior Software Engineer\n      EDUCATION  SKILLSFrontend & UX: React, TypeScript, Next.js, Storybook, CSS/SCSS, Design systems,Localisation, HTML5Backend & APIs: Node.js, Ruby on Rails, .NET, Python Flask,GraphQL, REST/JSON:API, Schema designTesting & Quality: Jest, React Testing Library (RTL), Cypress, RSpec, Chai, PuppeteerDevOps & Tooling: AWS, Docker, GitHub Actions, CircleCI, CI/CD, Monitoring, GitHubCollaboration & Leadership: Technical mentoring, Stakeholder facilitation, Accessibilityadvocacy, JIRA, Figma, Zeplin \nEDUCATION\nSKILLS\nFrontend & UX: React, TypeScript, Next.js, Storybook, CSS/SCSS, Design systems,\nLocalisation, HTML5\nBackend & APIs: Node.js, Ruby on Rails, .NET, Python Flask,\nGraphQL, REST/JSON:API, Schema design\nTesting & Quality: Jest, React Testing Library (RTL), Cypress, RSpec, Chai, Puppeteer\nDevOps & Tooling: AWS, Docker, GitHub Actions, CircleCI, CI/CD, Monitoring, GitHub\nCollaboration & Leadership: Technical mentoring, Stakeholder facilitation, Accessibility\nadvocacy, JIRA, Figma, Zeplin'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'MASTER_COVER_LETTER:\nLeonid Verman\nVancouver, BC, Canada · +1 (778) 317-3248 · leonidverman@gmail.com\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/in/leonid-verman-3569b71b2/\nFebruary 16h, 2026\nDear Hiring Manager,\nI am excited to apply for the Senior Software Engineer role at Tigera. With over 15 years of experience in backend engineering and team leadership, including roles as VP and Director of Software Development, I have built fintech and blockchain systems that became top-5 platforms in the Japanese market, serving over 1M users, and led teams of up to 20 engineers to deliver reliable, scalable products.\nIn addition to my industry work, I currently contribute as an independent contractor supporting world-leading AI research labs in training and evaluating advanced AI models on senior-level software engineering tasks. This work keeps me deeply engaged with cutting-edge development practices, reinforces my ability to solve complex problems under ambiguity, and strengthens my proficiency with AI-assisted development tools. I also adopt AI-accelerated workflows (ChatGPT, Claude, Cursor AI) to improve productivity, code quality, and architectural clarity.\nI would welcome the chance to bring this expertise to your team and contribute to building reliable, scalable systems for your clients. I hold Canadian citizenship and am fully eligible to work in Canada.\nThank you for your time and consideration.\n\nSincerely,\nLeonid Verman\n'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'TASK:\nTailor BOTH documents for:\n- Company: CARFAX\n- Role: Senior Software Engineer - Team Lead\n\nProduce a tailored resume and a tailored cover letter following the rules from the developer message.\nUse CURRENT_DATE: March 18, 2026\nReturn JSON only.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.2', 'response_format': {'type': 'json_object'}, 'temperature': 0.3}}
